exports[`characterization: current 907 rendered HTML is preserved > renders 2024-11-20-quick-thoughts.md identically 1`] = `
"<p>I've been thinking about attention lately. Not productivity hacks or focus techniques, but the deeper question of what it means to direct your awareness toward something.</p>
<h2>The Observation</h2>
<p>I notice that when I walk the same trail every day, I stop seeing it. My mind wanders. I'm physically present but mentally absent, running through todo lists or replaying conversations.</p>
<p>But then something breaks the pattern—unusual light, a bird call, fresh snow—and suddenly I'm <em>there</em> again. Fully present. The trail becomes new.</p>
<h2>The Question</h2>
<p>What if attention isn't something we have or lack, but something we practice? Like a muscle that atrophies without use?</p>
<p>The modern environment seems designed to scatter attention rather than cultivate it. Infinite feeds, constant notifications, the expectation of immediate response. Everything pulling us toward surface-level engagement.</p>
<h2>The Experiment</h2>
<p>For the past month, I've been leaving my phone at home during morning walks. Simple change, but the effect has been noticeable. Without the option to check messages or take photos, I just... walk. And notice things.</p>
<p>This morning: ice crystals on a dead fireweed stalk, catching the low sunlight. I would have missed it entirely with a screen in my pocket.</p>
<p>No grand conclusions yet. Just noticing what happens when I remove one small source of distraction.</p>
"
`;

exports[`characterization: current 907 rendered HTML is preserved > renders 2024-12-01-favorite-albums-2024.md identically 1`] = `
"<p>Every year I try to keep track of music that resonates. Here are a few albums from 2024 that I kept coming back to.</p>
<h2>The List</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Waxahatchee - Tigers Blood</strong>
Indie rock with a warm, lived-in feel. Perfect for long drives.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Mkwaju Ensemble - Mkwaju</strong>
Reissue of 1980s Kenyan electronic music. Hypnotic and strange in the best way.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Adrianne Lenker - Bright Future</strong>
Sparse, intimate folk. Her voice and guitar, mostly unadorned.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>SAULT - EARTH</strong>
Afrofuturist soul that feels both ancient and futuristic.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>The Smile - Wall of Eyes</strong>
Radiohead adjacent, but looser and more experimental.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>Themes</h2>
<p>Looking at this list, I realize I gravitated toward music that felt handmade and human this year. Less production gloss, more raw texture.</p>
<p>Maybe that's a reaction to how much time I spent staring at screens. Music became a refuge from the algorithmic feed.</p>
<p>What were your favorites this year?</p>
"
`;

exports[`characterization: current 907 rendered HTML is preserved > renders 2024-12-15-book-notes-example.md identically 1`] = `
"<p>Just finished Richard Powers' <em>The Overstory</em>. It's a sprawling novel that weaves together multiple storylines, all connected by trees and the people who love them.</p>
<h2>What Struck Me</h2>
<p>The book operates on multiple timescales simultaneously. There are human lives measured in decades, and tree lives measured in centuries. Powers shifts between them effortlessly, making you feel the weight of deep time.</p>
<p>The central question: What happens when you really pay attention to the living world around you?</p>
<h2>Favorite Passages</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>"The best arguments in the world won't change a person's mind. The only thing that can do that is a good story."</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This line kept coming back to me. Powers doesn't lecture about environmental destruction; he tells stories that make you see trees as characters with their own agency and narrative arcs.</p>
<h2>Recommended?</h2>
<p>Yes, especially if you're patient with literary fiction. It's long and slow-moving, and it rewards attention.</p>
"
`;

exports[`characterization: current 907 rendered HTML is preserved > renders 2025-01-10-winter-prior-lake.md identically 1`] = `
"<p>This morning I walked out to Prior Lake to catch the sunrise. At this time of year, the sun barely climbs above the horizon, painting the snow in shades of pink and gold.</p>
<p>The ice was thick enough to walk on, and the stillness was complete. No wind, no sound except the occasional creak of ice settling.</p>
<h2>The Light</h2>
<p>Winter light in Alaska has a quality I've never seen anywhere else. It's low and soft, almost horizontal, creating long shadows even at what passes for midday.</p>
<p>The temperature was around -15°F, cold enough that my breath crystallized instantly, but not so cold that I couldn't stay out with the camera for a while.</p>
<h2>Gear Notes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Camera: Still using the trusty old DSLR</li>
<li>Lens: 24-70mm</li>
<li>Extra batteries: Essential in the cold</li>
<li>Warm gloves with finger cutouts: essential</li>
</ul>
<p>The photos turned out better than expected. There's something about the simplicity of snow, ice, and light that makes photography feel pure again.</p>
"
`;

